diff --git a/docs/deployment.md b/docs/deployment.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..2831bde
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,191 @@
+# Deploying Fizzy with Kamal (single server)
+
+This guide deploys Fizzy to **one Linux box with Docker** using [Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org).
+Everything runs in a single container:
+
+- **SQLite** for all databases (`storage/production*.sqlite3`)
+- **Active Storage on local disk** (`storage/production/files`)
+- **Web + background jobs** together via Puma + Solid Queue (`SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA=true`)
+- **SSL** automatically via kamal-proxy (Let's Encrypt)
+
+Data survives redeploys because SQLite + files live in the `fizzy_storage` Docker volume.
+
+## Prerequisites
+
+On your workstation:
+
+- Docker running (to build the image)
+- Ruby + Bundler with `bundle install` (Kamal is already in the `Gemfile`)
+- SSH access to your server (as `root`, or a user that can run Docker)
+
+On your server:
+
+- A fresh Linux box (Ubuntu/Debian recommended) with SSH open
+- DNS for your hostname (e.g., `fizzy.example.com`) pointing at the server
+- Ports `80` and `443` reachable from the internet (for Let's Encrypt)
+
+On your mail provider:
+
+- SMTP credentials (host, port, username, password) for outbound email
+  (login codes, notifications, etc.)
+
+## 1. Fill in `config/deploy.yml`
+
+Open [`config/deploy.yml`](../config/deploy.yml) and replace every placeholder:
+
+| Key | Example | Notes |
+|-----|---------|-------|
+| `image` | `your-dockerhub-username/fizzy` | Must match `registry.username` prefix. For GHCR use `ghcr.io/your-user/fizzy` + `server: ghcr.io`. |
+| `servers.web` | `192.0.2.10` | Your server IP or hostname, reachable via SSH. |
+| `proxy.host` | `fizzy.example.com` | Public hostname. App will be at `https://<host>`. Must resolve to the server. |
+| `registry.username` | `your-dockerhub-username` | Docker Hub user (or `ghcr.io` user). |
+| `env.clear.APP_HOST` | `fizzy.example.com` | Must match `proxy.host`. Used for URLs in emails. |
+| `env.clear.SMTP_ADDRESS` | `smtp.example.com` | Your SMTP server. |
+| `env.clear.SMTP_PORT` | `587` | `587` (STARTTLS) or `465` (SSL) or `25` (local relay). |
+| `env.clear.SMTP_DOMAIN` | `fizzy.example.com` | Usually your app hostname (HELO domain). |
+| `env.clear.SMTP_USER_NAME` | `fizzy` | Leave blank for unauthenticated relays. |
+| `env.clear.MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS` | `Fizzy <fizzy@fizzy.example.com>` | From address for all mail. |
+
+Optional tuning in `env.clear` (commented by default):
+
+- `WEB_CONCURRENCY` / `JOB_CONCURRENCY` — Puma workers / Solid Queue processes (default: CPU count)
+- `MULTI_TENANT: "true"` — allow anyone to sign up; by default only the first account can be created
+
+No separate job server is needed: `SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA: true` runs jobs inside Puma.
+
+## 2. Fill in `.kamal/secrets`
+
+Open [`.kamal/secrets`](../.kamal/secrets). By default it reads from your shell environment:
+
+```sh
+# Generate each value once:
+bin/rails secret
+# => SECRET_KEY_BASE
+
+bin/rails runner 'k = WebPush.generate_key; puts "VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=#{k.private_key}\nVAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=#{k.public_key}"'
+# => VAPID keys for browser push notifications
+
+# Docker Hub: https://hub.docker.com/settings/security (read+write token)
+# => KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD
+
+# From your mail provider:
+# => SMTP_PASSWORD
+```
+
+Then export before running Kamal:
+
+```sh
+export SECRET_KEY_BASE="$(bin/rails secret)"
+export KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD="dckr_pat_..."
+export SMTP_PASSWORD="smtp-secret"
+export VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY="..."
+export VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY="..."
+
+# Verify Kamal can see the combined config (secrets are redacted):
+bin/kamal config
+```
+
+To use 1Password / LastPass / Bitwarden instead, see the commented examples in `.kamal/secrets`.
+
+> Never commit raw secrets. The default `.kamal/secrets` only contains `$VAR`
+> references, so it is safe for git. If you paste raw values there, add
+> `.kamal/secrets` to `.gitignore`.
+
+## 3. Deploy
+
+From the repo root:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+This will:
+
+1. Bootstrap Docker on the server (`kamal server bootstrap`)
+2. Build the image locally and push it to your registry
+3. Pull it on the server, create the `fizzy_storage` volume, and boot the app + proxy
+4. Provision a Let's Encrypt certificate for `proxy.host`
+
+Visit `https://your-host` when it finishes. The first visit lets you create the
+first account; further signups require an invite unless `MULTI_TENANT=true`.
+
+Later deploys are just:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal deploy
+```
+
+Check status with:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal app details
+bin/kamal app logs -f
+bin/kamal proxy logs
+```
+
+Open a console on the server:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal console
+bin/kamal shell
+```
+
+## How persistence works
+
+[`config/database.sqlite.yml`](../config/database.sqlite.yml) stores all production
+databases under `storage/`:
+
+- `storage/production.sqlite3` (primary)
+- `storage/production_cable.sqlite3`, `storage/production_cache.sqlite3`, `storage/production_queue.sqlite3`
+
+[`config/storage.oss.yml`](../config/storage.oss.yml) stores uploads under
+`storage/production/files` when `ACTIVE_STORAGE_SERVICE=local` (the default).
+
+`config/deploy.yml` mounts one volume for all of it:
+
+```yaml
+volumes:
+  - "fizzy_storage:/rails/storage"
+```
+
+On first boot the container runs `bin/rails db:prepare` via
+[`bin/docker-entrypoint`](../bin/docker-entrypoint), creating/migrating the
+databases inside the volume. Later deploys reuse the same volume, so data survives.
+
+To back up, snapshot the volume from the host:
+
+```sh
+ssh your-server-host "docker run --rm -v fizzy_storage:/data -v $(pwd):/backup ubuntu tar czf /backup/fizzy_storage_backup.tar.gz -C /data ."
+```
+
+## How jobs + mail + SSL work
+
+- **Jobs:** [`config/puma.rb`](../config/puma.rb) loads `plugin :solid_queue`
+  unless `SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA=false` or SaaS mode. With `SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA=true`
+  in `deploy.yml`, the web container runs the Solid Queue supervisor + recurring
+  jobs (`config/queue.yml`, `config/recurring.yml`) on the box.
+- **Mail:** [`config/environments/production.rb`](../config/environments/production.rb)
+  configures `action_mailer.smtp_settings` from `SMTP_*` and
+  `default_url_options` from `APP_HOST` (fallback `APP_FQDN`/`HOST`).
+  `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS` sets the From address (see `app/mailers/application_mailer.rb`).
+- **SSL:** `proxy.ssl: true` makes kamal-proxy fetch a Let's Encrypt certificate
+  for `proxy.host` and redirect HTTP → HTTPS. Rails already sets
+  `assume_ssl`/`force_ssl` by default in production, and `/up` is excluded from
+  host authorization for healthchecks.
+
+## Troubleshooting
+
+- **`bin/kamal config` fails with “Secret not found”:** ensure the names in
+  `env/secret` + `registry/password` exist in `.kamal/secrets` and that the
+  corresponding `$VAR` is exported (or your password-manager fetch works).
+- **SSL / Let's Encrypt fails:** confirm DNS points at the server, ports 80/443
+  are open, and `proxy.host` == `APP_HOST`. Check `bin/kamal proxy logs`.
+- **Healthcheck fails (`/up`):** check `bin/kamal app logs`. Common causes are
+  missing `SECRET_KEY_BASE` or a bad `SMTP_*` value preventing boot.
+- **Permission denied on `/rails/storage`:** the image runs as user `rails`
+  (uid 1000). Named volumes initialize with the correct owner on first deploy;
+  if you pre-created the volume as root, remove it (`docker volume rm fizzy_storage`)
+  and redeploy (this deletes data — back up first).
+- **Push notifications don’t work:** ensure both `VAPID_*` keys are set and
+  redeploy. To disable push, remove both lines from `env/secret` instead of
+  leaving them blank.
